$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The petitioner is serving a life sentence in a 2010 murder case from Paharganj. Earlier this year, the High Court set aside the rejection of his first furlough application and granted him 21 days, which he used — released on 7 October 2026, due back on 28 October — to begin repairs on his dilapidated ancestral house. The work could not realistically finish in three weeks. With no family member in Delhi to supervise the remainder, he applied to the authorities for an extension; the representation sat undecided as the surrender date approached, and he moved the writ court.</p>

<h2>The State\'s objection: the Rules are silent</h2>
<p>The State\'s position had the appeal of textual tidiness: the Delhi Prison Rules, 2018 provide for spells of furlough but contain no provision for extending one, so a released convict must surrender and, in due course, seek his next spell. The argument has succeeded before and was supported by an earlier single-judge order. Justice Sachin Datta took the other line of authority. Rules 1197 and 1200 frame parole and furlough as progressive measures of correctional service, humanistic in purpose — language the Supreme Court\'s decision in <em>Asfaq v. State of Rajasthan</em> reinforces. Against that purposive backdrop, coordinate Benches of the Delhi High Court — in <em>Dinesh Kumar</em>, <em>Jasbir Kaur</em>, <em>Ramesh Dudhiya</em> and <em>Pradeep Kumar @ Sonu</em> — have extended or re-granted furlough in emergent circumstances, relaxing where necessary the usual one-month interval between spells under the Standing Order. The absence of an express extension provision, the Court held, places no embargo on the High Court extending furlough in appropriate circumstances.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The doctrinal point travels beyond prisons: where delegated rules confer a beneficial, rehabilitative facility and are silent on an incidental power, the silence is read in service of the facility\'s purpose, not against the beneficiary. The Court\'s citation of a Punjab &amp; Haryana order extending furlough for monsoon-damage repairs shows the approach is not idiosyncratic to Delhi.</p>
</div>

<h2>The order and its safeguards</h2>
<p>The furlough was extended by four weeks on the same conditions as originally imposed, with surrender obligatory at the end of the extended period. Critically, the extension is not free liberty: it will be adjusted against the petitioner\'s furlough entitlement for the conviction year ending in February 2027. The convict, in other words, spends his annual allowance in one longer spell rather than several shorter ones — a structure that preserves the incentive architecture of the Rules while meeting the practical need.</p>

<h2>What applicants should take from the judgment</h2>
<p>Three things. First, timing: the petitioner applied to the authorities for extension well before his surrender date and approached the Court when the representation remained pending — a sequence that avoids any taint of overstay, which would itself be misconduct affecting future spells. Second, specificity: the purpose was concrete, verifiable and time-bound — a dilapidated house, an estimated three to four weeks of supervised work, no family in Delhi to stand in. Vague pleas of personal work do not move courts; a defined task with a defined horizon can. Third, realism about the cost: because extensions are adjusted against the annual entitlement, counsel should weigh whether the client is better served by surrendering and seeking the next spell later in the year. Furlough jurisprudence in Delhi is now substantially judge-made at its edges, and this order adds a clear, citable holding: silence in the Rules is not a wall.</p>
